592186045417] [17592186121780 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121780 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8ebd6d4-ef22-55d1-b2fd-1d2f280fa42a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121781 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121781 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121781 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5b1a9ac3-b9a3-59ca-891f-c8d93f27fd0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121782 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121782 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121782 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "62f0ec41-6c5f-51cb-9439-22250619fa40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121783 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121783 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121783 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "730f0ca8-ac97-51bf-ae0d-7facf7eedff8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124320 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124320 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n![](images/6a/6a294de4a5f2ff5755d8ceb5b91bb8920685a1cf2b5e727c5b5fdcfaaf0b89b0.svg)\n\nI. $A$ is not invertible\n\nII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124320 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "266a9a4e-cb11-5e4a-a2ac-c2e734966372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124320 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n![](images/4b/4b42fd4ec8ccf491a1244a648af6fe40ce846fad388685e36d499783a095f8f7.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n![](images/32/327a582d15b82ebccda48358062310e6ad5c3bb2689f2024d76a8f8e7e1401a0.svg)\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124433 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124433 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\det(A) = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\nIII. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1120305-c8cb-5f57-97b1-9b31dc059b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124433 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](images/dc/dc72aea1969143a926fd637d3360bb42116b27545a748923069f4ae732bd700f.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124434 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124434 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3e/3e58a5f09c9060932ee854feeec0733dc9432c2de658308e3ebdccdd0ef89e5f.svg)\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fff1daf8-5a1f-54c9-90d7-87710ca00d23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124434 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/21/21455a41af5743da6f9f4de5e267c17c6037b929a01ba894cfd91530dbb39986.svg)\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186124870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124511